module v2.test.claim.self_host.negate_wrapped_operand_subject_fixture

// INNER-REFUSAL CONTROL (neat-badger's added gate on the operand-render widening). The OUTER apply is a
// realisable binary arithmetic use `y + (-x)` (op_add HAS a Rust realization row), and its RIGHT operand is a
// PREFIX use of an operation the catalog cannot spell -- `-x`, canonical_operation_op_negate, a roster member
// with NO operator-realization row (the same op the standalone prefix_negate_operand_subject exercises). If the
// lowering genuinely DESCENDS into the operand, this subject refuses with the SAME inner realization/arity reason
// the standalone negate refuses with. A SHALLOW implementation would instead coerce the `-x` operand to a binding
// Symbol and report the OUTER operand-binding reason, or fall back to the generic transform-shape bucket -- so the
// claim that this subject's refusal EQUALS the standalone negate's refusal (exact, not containment) is the single
// strongest discriminator of real recursion. Lives under the self-host claim tree, NOT the production corpus.
fn negate_wrapped_operand_subject(x: Int, y: Int) -> Int {
  y + (-x)
}
